[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m If no one shares an office, in how many ways can 3 people be assigned to 5 different offices? (Each person gets exactly one office).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To determine the number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices, we need to consider that each person can be assigned to any of the 5 offices independently of the others. This is a problem of distributing 3 distinct items (people) into 5 distinct bins (offices).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The number of ways to do this is given by the number of permutations of 3 items taken 5 at a time, which is calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 5! = 5 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Let's compute this step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 5! = 5 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 120
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Therefore, the number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices is \(\boxed{120}\).<|endoftext|>
