[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To determine how many work days it will take three painters to finish the job, we need to first find out the total amount of work in terms of painter-days. We know that 4 painters take 1.25 days to complete the job. Therefore, the total amount of work in painter-days can be calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 4 \text{ painters} \times 1.25 \text{ days} = 5 \text{ painter-days}
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m This means the job requires 5 painter-days to complete. Next, we need to find out how many work days 3 painters will take to complete 5 painter-days of work. Let's call the number of work days required for 3 painters to complete the job \( x \). Therefore, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m x \times 3 \text{ painters} = 5 \text{ painter-days}
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Solving for \( x \), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m x = \frac{5 \text{ painter-days}}{3 \text{ painters}} = \frac{5}{3} \text{ days}
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Now, we need to express \(\frac{5}{3}\) as a mixed number. The whole number part of \(\frac{5}{3}\) is 1, and the fractional part is \(\frac{2}{3}\). Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 1 \frac{2}{3} \text{ days}
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m So, the number of work-days it will take three painters to finish the job is:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[
