[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To solve this problem, we need to distribute the marbles from Lisa's 34 marbles among her 10 friends such that each friend gets at least one marble and no two friends get the same number of marbles.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 1. **Initial Distribution**: Since Lisa has 10 friends, the minimum number of marbles she needs is always 10. This is because each friend gets at least one marble.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 2. **Ensure Each Friend has at Least One Marble**: To ensure each friend has at least one marble, we need to give at least 1 marble to each of the 10 friends.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 3. **Distribute Marbles Remaining**: After giving each friend 1 marble, Lisa has distributed \(10\) marbles in total. She still needs \(34 - 10 = 24\) more marbles to make up the $24 - 34 (since each of her 10 friends gets 1 marble) marbles. 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 4. **Optimizing Distribution**: To minimize the number of additional marbles needed, we can give the last remaining marble to one of the remaining friends who might have fewer marbles. However, since we are distributing marbles already, it is usually simpler to keep the last marble for Lisa herself, as she is not sharing one of her marbles.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Therefore, the minimum number of additional marbles Lisa needs to give to each friend to ensure each friend gets at least one marble and no two friends receive the same number of marbles is \(\boxed{1}\).
