[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To solve for the values of \( x \) such that \( f(x) = 0 \), we need to consider the two possible cases for the function \( f(x) \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ f(x) = 2x + 1 \quad \text{and} \quad f(x) = 8 - 4x. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m We will solve each case separately.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 3. For \( 2x + 1 = 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    2x + 1 = 0 \implies 2x = -1 \implies x = -\frac{1}{2}.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m We will check if \( x = -\frac{1}{2} \) falls within the domain of \( f(x) \), which is \( x \le 3 \). Since \( -\frac{1}{2} = -0.5 \) is indeed less than or equal to 3, it is a valid solution for \( x \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 4. For \( 8 - 4x = 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    8 - 4x = 0 \implies 4x = 8 \implies x = 2.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m We will check if \( x = 2 \) falls within the domain of \( f(x) \), which is \( x > 3 \). Since \( 2 > 3 \) is false, \( x = 2 \) is not a valid solution for \( x \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m So, the only valid solution is \( x = -\frac{1}{2} \). The sum of all values of \( x \) that satisfy \( f(x) = 0 \) is simply the value of \( x \) that we found, which is \( -\frac{1}{2} \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Therefore, the sum of all values of \( x \) such that \( f(x) = 0 \) is \(\boxed{-\frac{1}{2}}\).<|endoftext|>
