[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Zembury Stele, inscription and historical background of the stele in the life of the country
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The Zembury Stele, also known as the Japanese Imperial Edict Stele or Mishonum Shoku, is a stele dedicated by the Imperial Edict of Autoaffiburo to the Emperor Kōkyú's parents, which is entombed in the Eastern section of the Imperial Vorterra Shima Shima Shrine in the Minamoto Kaiji Nannori Shrine of Japan, situated in Rikugyo. The stele stands 1 metre 9 centimeters.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The ninety-nine pillars, also known as hodoi no shonin shōjo, from seven horizontal lines and ten vertical brackets, are arranged in a unique pattern.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The scroll and inscription was consecutively presented to the emperor by the Kaitōyon dedicated by His Grace Emperor Yoshihiko. Emperor Hirohito was specifically requested to also dedicate the stele and set it up during visits to Kyoto in the seventh year of the reign of His Grace Emperor Kanze (1779-1785). The kahaku whose responsibilities corresponded to those of the emperor was also one of the honored guests that Emperor Hirohito officiated for on the stele. The premiere of Zomburuyoku Chojenin Fujivalitoku Ohishi (皇族ご親国君求拜書) occurred on March 26, 1783 at 5:30 p.m. Kawaguchi Nihon Yōkuro Sekiya, the last shrine visited by the Kaitōyon, housed the stele until the fire that ripped the shrine and Lettuce Kaishou was discovered on April 25, 1783.
