[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To solve for \( b \) in the equation \( y = a \sin (bx + c) \), we need to understand the behavior of the sine function and how it is scaled by \( a \) and \( b \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The general form of the sine function is:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ y = a \sin (bx + c) \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m For this function to be well-defined, the argument of the sine function, \( bx + c \), must be of the form \( 2k\pi \) for some integer \( k \). This occurs when:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ bx + c = 2k\pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m for some integer \( k \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Given the equations at the right of the graph, where the graph crosses the line \( y = 2 \) and at \( y = -2 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 1. At \( y = 2 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ 2 = a \sin (b(x) + c) \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    Since \( \sin (b(x) + c) \) will always be between -1 and 1, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ a \sin (b(x) + c) = 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ a \cdot 2 = 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ a = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 2. At \( y = -2 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ -2 = a \sin (b(x) + c) \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    Similarly, \[ a \sin (b(x) + c) = -2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ a \cdot (-2) = -2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m    \[ a = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Both conditions cannot be satisfied by the same \( a \). Therefore, the function \( y = 1 \sin (bx + c) \) does not intersect the line \( y = 2 \) and \( y = -2 \) on the same side of the origin. This implies that the ratio of \( b \) to \( x \) must vary such that the vertical asymptote of the sine function is within the interval where the sine function is continuous and periodic. The sine function \( \sin (bx) \) reaches its maximum value of 1 when \( bx = \frac{\pi}{2} + 2k\pi \) and its minimum value of -1 when \( bx = -\frac{\pi}{2} + 2k\pi \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m For the sine function to be continuous and periodic, the vertical asymptotes must be at \( x = \frac{\pi}{2} + k\pi \). This implies:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ b = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Thus, the value of \( b \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \boxed{1} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
