[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To find the domain of the function \( f(x) = \tan(\arccos(x^2)) \), we need to ensure that the argument of the \(\arccos\) function, \( x^2 \), lies within the domain of the \(\arccos\) function, which is \([-1, 1]\). The expression inside the \(\arccos\) function must be in the interval \([-1, 1]\).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m So, we need to solve the inequality:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ -1 \leq x^2 \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m First, consider the left part of the inequality:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ -1 \leq x^2. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m This inequality holds when:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ -1 \leq x^2 \quad \text{and} \quad x^2 \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m From the second part, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ x^2 \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m This means:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ -1 \leq x \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Next, consider the right part of the inequality:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ x^2 \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m This inequality holds when:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ x^2 \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m This means:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ -1 \leq x \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Combining these two parts, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ -1 \leq x \leq 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Therefore, the domain of the function \( f(x) = \tan(\arccos(x^2)) \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \boxed{[-1, 1]}. \]<|endoftext|>
