[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To determine the volume of the new container, we start by understanding the relationship between the dimensions of the original container and the new container. The original container has dimensions of 1 foot in width, 1 foot in height, and 1 foot in depth. This translates to a volume of:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{Volume of the original container} = 1 \text{ ft} \times 1 \text{ ft} \times 1 \text{ ft} = 1 \text{ cubic foot} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m When we triple the dimensions of the original container, the new dimensions become:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{New width} = 1 \text{ ft} \times 3 = 3 \text{ feet} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{New height} = 1 \text{ ft} \times 3 = 3 \text{ feet} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{New depth} = 1 \text{ ft} \times 3 = 3 \text{ feet} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The volume of the new container is calculated by multiplying the new dimensions:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{Volume of the new container} = 3 \text{ ft} \times 3 \text{ ft} \times 3 \text{ ft} = 27 \text{ cubic feet} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Since there are 7.48 gallons in one cubic foot, the volume of the new container is:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{Volume of the new container} = 27 \text{ cubic feet} \times 7.48 \text{ gallons per cubic foot} = 206.86 \text{ gallons} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Therefore, the new container holds \(\boxed{206.86}\) gallons.<|endoftext|>
