[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Evaluate $i^{11} + i^{111}$.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To evaluate \( i^{11} + i^{111} \), we need to understand the cyclical nature of the powers of the imaginary unit \( i \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m The powers of \( i \) cycle every 4 terms in the following arithmetic sequence:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^1 = i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^2 = -1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^3 = -i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^4 = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^5 = i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ \text{and so on.} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m So, to find \( i^n \), we can determine the remainder when \( n \) is divided by 4. This remainder will tell us the periodicity starting from \( i^1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m First, we find the remainders for \( 11 \) and \( 111 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ 11 \mod 4 = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ 111 \mod 4 = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Now, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^{11} = i^3 = -i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^{111} = i^{3 \cdot 37 + 2} = (i^3)^{37} \cdot i^2 = (-i)^{37} \cdot (-1) = -i^{37} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Since \( (-i)^4 = 1 \) and \( k \cdot 180^\circ \mod 360^\circ \approx 37^\circ \), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^{37} = i^{37 \mod 360} = i^{37} = i^{37 \mod 4 \cdot 360} = i^3 = -i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m So:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[ i^{111} = -i \cdot (-1) = i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Therefore, the expression \( i^{11} + i^{111} = -i + i = 0 \). We can write this in Python to confirm our reasoning.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m # Function to calculate (i^n + i^m) where i = 1i
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m def calculate_power_mod_powers():
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     # Powers of i cycle every 4 terms: i, -1, -i, 1
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     powers = [1, -1, -1, 1]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     # We need to find (i^11 + i^111) for the specific remainder 3
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     remainder = (11 % 4 + 1) % 4  # (11 is included in modulo 4) + 1
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     power_11 = powers[remainder]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     power_111 = powers[remainder + coordinates[remainder]]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     result = power_11 + power_111
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m     return result
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m # Initialize variables
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m complex_number = 3 + 4j
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m coordinates = [int(s) for s in complex_number.components()]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m # Calculate (i^11 + i^111)
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m result = calculate_power_mod_powers()
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m print(result)
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m ```
