[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m To find the maximum value of the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\), we will use the Euclidean algorithm. Let \(d = \gcd(11n + 3, 6n + 1)\). By the properties of the GCD, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[d \mid (11n + 3) \quad \text{and} \quad d \mid (6n + 1)\]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m This means that \(d\) also divides any linear combination of these two numbers. In particular, we can express \(d\) as:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[d \mid (11n + 3) - 6(6n + 1)\]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Performing the division, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[d \mid (11n + 3) - 6(6n + 1) = 11n + 3 - 36n - 6 = -15n - 3\]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[d \mid 3(-5n - 1)\]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m So, \(d \mid 3(-5n - 1)\). Since \(-5n - 1\) is not necessarily divisible by 3, \(d\) cannot be 1, 2, or 3. We need to check if \(d\) can be greater than 3.
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Consider the case when \(d = 6\). Checking if \(d = 6\) divides both \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\), we can use the Euclidean algorithm again:
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[11n + 3 = 6(5n + 1) + 3\]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m \[6n + 1 = 11n + 3 - 6n - 3 = 5n + 3\]
[36m(main_task pid=3102311)[0m Since \(d\) divides both expressions, \(d \mid 3\). For \(d\) to be a divisor of 3, it must be 1 or 3. Thus, the maximum possible value for the GCD is 3, and it occurs when \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\) are女排.celonaous absent from all methods.
